You can create a link for each post on your website by adding a `location` block in your Nginx configuration file. This block will serve as a redirector for each post. 

Here is an example of how you can do this:

```nginx
location /posts/(.*) {
    rewrite ^ /posts/detail/$1 permanent;
}
```

This will rewrite any request to `/posts/*` (for example `/posts/post1`, `/posts/post2`, and so on) to `/posts/detail/*`. You will need to create a new location block for the `/posts/detail/*` path that serves the desired content.

For example:

```nginx
location /posts/detail/(.*) {
    root /usr/share/nginx/html;
    index detail.html;
}
```

This assumes that you have a `detail.html` template file in the `/usr/share/nginx/html/posts` directory that will serve as the template for each post.

You can also use variables in the `location` block to make the links more dynamic:

```nginx
location /posts/(.*) {
    rewrite ^ /posts/detail/$1 permanent;
}
```

And then in the `detail` block:

```nginx
location /posts/detail/(.*) {
    root /usr/share/nginx/html;
    index detail.html;
    include /path/to/variables.txt;
}
```

Where `variables.txt` contains variables such as `$post_id`, `$post_permalink`, and so on.

You can also create different types of links (internal links, external links, etc.) by modifying the `location` block to suit your needs. 

But remember that the nginx configuration is a binary format and it doesn't support dynamic programming. Any dynamic operation should be done in an upstream server like PHP, Java, Python etc.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

